# The root R0 without an entry. A declaration with a literal of a Structure written in the data of a function is a place
# of the code, local to each launch: `int: x 5` gives x 5 every time, also after the body changed it (x := x + 1), and
# the source keeps the written 5 (Log\source 5). The Structure, called twice by the one-argument form of the receiver,
# runs over its actual parent -- the data of the function --, so `data\x` is the place x of the parent: the second launch
# enters with 5 (Log\entry 5), and the function returns 0 + 5 + 5 (Log\parent 10).
# Norm: the book #structural-call (the data is parent(C)), the roles of data and code (#data-code-roles: a code
# declaration «checks the type on its own line and, when a value is written, assigns … without extending source data»).
@: Log (int: parent 0; int: entry 0; int: source 0)
fn: h () int (
    int: x 0
    Self(int: x 5; Log\entry := data\x; data\x := data\x + x; x := x + 1)
)
    call(code := Self)
    call(code := Self)
    return: data\x
Log\parent := h()
Log\source := h\data\Self\x
